WEBVTT

00:00:00.105 --> 00:00:01.855
Most databases are passive:

00:00:02.289 --> 00:00:04.658
they sit and wait until you ask them

00:00:04.658 --> 00:00:05.132
something.

00:00:05.566 --> 00:00:08.500
Triggers turn them into active databases

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.289
that can react to changes.

00:00:11.122 --> 00:00:12.885
A trigger is a set of actions that the

00:00:12.898 --> 00:00:15.398
database runs when someone inserts,

00:00:15.793 --> 00:00:18.977
updates or deletes data in one specific

00:00:18.991 --> 00:00:19.385
table.

00:00:20.221 --> 00:00:24.761
The story starts in 1975 with the idea of

00:00:24.761 --> 00:00:26.458
integrity constraints.

00:00:26.458 --> 00:00:30.340
Constraints became part of SQL-92, and

00:00:30.603 --> 00:00:32.195
triggers, also called active rules,

00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:34.866
arrived in SQL-99.

00:00:35.313 --> 00:00:37.879
Products support them in widely varying

00:00:37.879 --> 00:00:40.563
ways, with different execution semantics.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:44.035
Every trigger follows the same recipe:

00:00:44.522 --> 00:00:46.377
when an event happens, if a condition is

00:00:46.377 --> 00:00:48.311
true, then do an action.

00:00:48.732 --> 00:00:51.469
The event is usually a change to the data.

00:00:51.877 --> 00:00:54.153
The condition is a test on the situation.

00:00:54.574 --> 00:00:56.732
The action is an update statement or a

00:00:56.745 --> 00:00:59.311
stored procedure, and it can also raise an

00:00:59.324 --> 00:01:00.311
error notification.

00:01:01.172 --> 00:01:03.343
Here is an example with an Exam table.

00:01:03.764 --> 00:01:05.185
We want to keep a log of alerts when

00:01:05.185 --> 00:01:06.474
grades are updated.

00:01:06.908 --> 00:01:09.540
The event is an update of Grade in Exam,

00:01:10.066 --> 00:01:11.843
and the action is an insert into the

00:01:11.843 --> 00:01:12.632
Alerts table.

00:01:13.066 --> 00:01:15.329
The condition sits in the middle and

00:01:15.329 --> 00:01:17.724
decides whether an alert is really needed.

00:01:18.559 --> 00:01:21.020
Triggers run in addition to integrity

00:01:21.033 --> 00:01:23.296
constraints, and they let you check more

00:01:23.296 --> 00:01:24.599
complex conditions.

00:01:25.007 --> 00:01:27.125
They are compiled and stored inside the

00:01:27.138 --> 00:01:28.086
DBMS.

00:01:28.941 --> 00:01:29.902
Now the big picture.

00:01:30.336 --> 00:01:32.625
One UPDATE statement can change several

00:01:32.625 --> 00:01:34.889
rows, here two exam rows.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:37.757
The statement activates the rules, and

00:01:37.902 --> 00:01:40.047
each rule can look at the data before and

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:41.625
after the change through transition

00:01:41.639 --> 00:01:42.362
variables.
